00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.780
What are neural scaling laws?

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:06.970
It’s those curves that show the dependency
between the error rate of a model, and the

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:11.841
amount of training data or the model size,
or the compute.

00:03:11.841 --> 00:03:17.060
Recent work has all shown that the neural
scaling laws follow a power law, which looks

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:18.100
like this.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:22.880
We see in this formula, that to reduce the
loss, we should add more data points, but

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.300
how much these data points help, depends on
this factor nu here.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:31.209
The nu factor is problem- and model dependent.

00:03:31.209 --> 00:03:36.020
A power law with a small nu, flattens quickly,
while a nu of 1, plateaus later.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:39.050
And this means that, we cite

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:44.960
“for large vision transformers, an additional
2 billion pre-training data points (starting

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:51.599
from 1 billion) leads to an accuracy gain
on ImageNet of a few percentage points”,

00:03:51.599 --> 00:03:57.180
so two billion to a few percentage points,
that is very little, because vision transformers

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.880
follow a power law which plateau very quickly.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:06.250
In log space, power laws look like a line,
as we see the dark line in this figure.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:12.260
Only that an exponent of -1 is happening only
for the idealized case of a perceptron working

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:18.251
on infinitely-dimensional data, so in reality,
these exponents are much smaller, something

00:04:18.251 --> 00:04:24.639
like 0.095 for a transformer language model,
which means that this curve here, is much,

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:26.050
much flatter.

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:30.789
If we want to reduce the cross-entropy loss
by not even one nat, we require an order of

00:04:30.789 --> 00:04:31.870
magnitude more data.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:36.479
By the way, a “nat” is like a bit but
when using the natural logarithm and not the

00:04:36.479 --> 00:04:37.860
basis of two.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:44.889
So, the idea of this paper is to develop a
theory about how to make the loss reduce faster

00:04:44.889 --> 00:04:46.370
than a power law.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:52.270
We can do this by determining which of the
data points are redundant and uninformative

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:53.909
and discard them,

00:04:53.909 --> 00:04:59.289
such that by only training on the most informative
data points, we can achieve the same error

00:04:59.289 --> 00:05:02.550
rate but with fewer data points in total!

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:07.710
This would make the dependency between error
rate and amounts of training data an exponential

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:12.889
dependency, which is decaying faster than
power laws, as you can see in this plot in

00:05:12.889 --> 00:05:13.889
the light curves.

00:05:13.889 --> 00:05:19.499
Okay, then let’s follow how the authors
reduce the power law to an exponential scaling

00:05:19.499 --> 00:05:22.900
law in theory and then we will follow their
experiments.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:29.949
First, we enter the world of math, so we are
in the limit of infinite training data (basically

00:05:29.949 --> 00:05:32.810
the dream of every machine learning researcher)

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:38.330
and to make everything tractable, our dataset
is in the limit infinitely dimensional.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:42.550
This means our perceptron model which works
on these data points, has infinite weights

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:43.669
to learn.

00:05:43.669 --> 00:05:44.669
Easy enough.

00:05:44.669 --> 00:05:45.669
¯\_(ツ)_/¯

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:49.279
But the idea is that the number of data points
is just as infinite as the dimensionality

00:05:49.279 --> 00:05:50.469
of the data.

00:05:50.469 --> 00:05:55.610
Then the idea of pruning is the following:
We have a teacher perceptron which learns

00:05:55.610 --> 00:06:02.089
to label our training data which is independent
and identically distributed and has unit variance

00:06:02.089 --> 00:06:05.529
– again, to make it easy enough for the
maths to work.

00:06:05.529 --> 00:06:10.960
Then we learn the weights of a student perceptron
for a few epochs on the training data, so

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.550
imagine this student to be a bit undertrained.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:20.169
Then we basically compare the distances between
the decision boundary of the teacher and the

00:06:20.169 --> 00:06:22.539
decision boundary of the student.

00:06:22.539 --> 00:06:23.729
How?

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:29.219
We take a point and look at the logit of the
classification, which gives us the distance

00:06:29.219 --> 00:06:31.969
do the decision boundary of the student.

00:06:31.969 --> 00:06:36.699
Then we look at the logit of the teacher,
so now we have this distance.

00:06:36.699 --> 00:06:42.830
By subtracting the two distances, we can estimate
the margin, so distance between the two decision

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:45.270
boundaries for each point.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:51.509
The authors define points with large margins
to be easy and points with small margins to

00:06:51.509 --> 00:06:52.589
be hard.

00:06:52.589 --> 00:06:57.849
This is because, in the easy examples, the
margin of error is big,

00:06:57.849 --> 00:07:03.169
so we have a lot of space to move the yellow
line left or right while still classifying

00:07:03.169 --> 00:07:04.740
the point correctly.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:10.029
In the hard examples, the margin of error
before misclassifying and placing the yellow

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:13.929
line on the incorrect side of the teacher
model, is small.

00:07:13.929 --> 00:07:15.969
So, those are hard examples.

00:07:15.969 --> 00:07:17.110
Okay then.

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:22.389
To prune the dataset, the authors discard
a fraction f of the hardest examples and a

00:07:22.389 --> 00:07:27.830
new perceptron can be trained on the pruned
dataset, not containing the easy examples.

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:33.649
Doing the maths, the authors can estimate
what exactly is the error added by pruning,

00:07:33.649 --> 00:07:38.669
so by removing uninformative training data
and can determine conditions such that this

00:07:38.669 --> 00:07:40.349
error is minimal.

00:07:40.349 --> 00:07:42.229
Interesting lessons follow from this:

00:07:42.229 --> 00:07:48.669
First, it follows that it is not always best
to discard the easiest examples, but that

00:07:48.669 --> 00:07:54.089
“The best pruning strategy depends on the
amount of initial data”.

00:07:54.089 --> 00:07:58.651
Suppose we have a large dataset that we want
to prune, then it is ok to discard the easy

00:07:58.651 --> 00:08:01.339
examples and train on the hard examples.

00:08:01.339 --> 00:08:06.490
Training data is still abundant in this case,
because remember, we start from a lot of data

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:11.599
and after we delete something like 30% of
it, we are still in a large dataset regime.

00:08:11.599 --> 00:08:17.349
But if we have a small dataset, it is best
to discard the hard examples and retain the

00:08:17.349 --> 00:08:18.349
easy ones.

00:08:18.349 --> 00:08:24.509
And this makes sense, because if we prune
a small dataset, we make it even smaller and

00:08:24.509 --> 00:08:26.899
the model will quickly overfit.

00:08:26.899 --> 00:08:31.209
With little data, you have to compromise,
since you do not have a chance to solve the

00:08:31.209 --> 00:08:36.240
hard points without overfitting anyways, so
it is better to keep the easy examples and

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.900
train on them because you might be able to
generalize with them.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.830
Okay, but this is what the theory predicts,
does it align with experiments?

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:50.210
Yes, it kind of does, at least for this ResNet18
trained on CIFAR-10, where we see the predicted

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:56.850
pattern that for not so many total examples,
the accuracy is higher when keeping easy examples

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.960
than hard ones (here in blue).

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:04.570
The next lesson is that if the pruning algorithm
were optimal, the power law scaling can transition

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:07.560
perfectly into exponential scaling.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:11.560
But the third lesson is that in practice,
we do not have a perfect pruning metric to

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:18.640
decide what examples are easy and which ones
are hard so, we cite “An imperfect pruning

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:23.060
metric yields a cross over from exponential
to power law scaling”.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:26.710
We are not exactly exponential, but we are
getting there.

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:27.710
;)

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:30.380
So how to think about all this from an information-theoretic
perspective?

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:34.990
We cite, “data pruning can increase the
information gained per example by pruning

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:37.580
away the uninformative examples.”

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:43.120
This means that if we have a normal dataset,
a lot of examples are not so new and deliver

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:45.540
redundant information.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:50.470
Pruning could determine the right examples
which are worthy to learn from.

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:55.420
Imagine a pruning method that could tell in
advance whether data points are worthy to

00:09:55.420 --> 00:10:00.610
consider in supervised learning, so whether
they are worthy to annotate.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:05.510
But already existing pruning metrics, work
with the labels of the data, therefore only

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:09.820
apply after the data has already been annotated.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:14.720
Such a method is for example the memorization
method measuring how much the probability

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:20.730
of predicting the correct label for a sample
increases after it was included in the training

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:21.730
set.

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:26.650
But so far, these supervised data pruning
metrics are proposed in papers working on

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:31.220
small datasets and do not apply to larger
benchmarks, such as ImageNet.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:36.630
A contribution of this paper is that the authors
run experiments to benchmark supervised pruning

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:42.080
methods, so if you are interested in this,
then look into figure 5 in the paper.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:47.390
It would be really great though, if we would
have an unsupervised method that would tell

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:52.970
which data to keep and which to prune, without
using the labels.

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:57.900
But wait no more, this paper casually delivers
one exactly as required, so we can use it

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:02.740
in advance of data annotation to determine
which data is worthy of labelling and which

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:07.360
not, before requesting costly annotations
from humans!

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.670
The main idea of the method is really simple:

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:15.800
To prune a dataset, like they do with ImageNet,
they use an already existing pretrained model,

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:19.110
such as SWaV to simply represent the data.

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:23.830
So they run the images through this pretrained
model, just to get a vector, so the position

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:28.410
of each data point in the representation space
of this SWaV model.

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:32.110
If you are not familiar with this model, just
imagine it is a powerful pretrained model

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:34.990
capable of generating good image representations.

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:40.680
Then, the pruning method cannot get any simpler
than this the authors run a k-means clustering

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:47.350
algorithm to cluster all the data into k clusters
(ok, choosing this hyperparameter can be tricky,

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:48.970
but the rest of the method is simple).

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:54.170
So now we have centroids, so cluster centers
and points further away from them.

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:58.660
Following the idea from theory, that hard
examples are close to the decision boundary

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:03.420
and simple points are far away from it, the
authors define a hard example to be far away

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:09.060
from cluster centers and simple examples to
be more prototypical, so closer to cluster

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:10.280
centers.

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:12.030
And that is the idea.

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:17.360
The experiments they run with a ResNet trained
on pruned versions of ImageNet are promising.

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:22.820
We want to highlight that the top-5 accuracy
on ImageNet of a ResNet trained on just 80%

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:27.770
of ImageNet, matches the accuracy of a ResNet
trained on the entire dataset.

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:33.160
This unsupervised pruning method also does
well when compared to supervised methods,

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:35.680
such as memorization.

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:37.680
What do you think of this paper?

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:40.760
Are you as excited about this as we are?

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:45.600
We have to say that (as it is the case with
most of the topics we cover on this channel),

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.540
we presented what we understood from this
paper.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:53.981
So, maybe you are more knowledgeable about
the topic of pruning or active learning, so

00:12:53.981 --> 00:12:58.320
let us know what you think and highlight all
the things we might have missed.

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:03.480
And if you are not an expert on this field,
we hope you got an interesting little insight,

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.570
just as we did while making this video!

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:09.470
Okay, bye!

